@* DeactivateUserDialog.razor — second-step confirmation modal for user
   deactivation.

   OPENED BY:
     AdminUsers.razor's HandleDeactivateAsync and UserDetail.razor's
     HandleDeactivateAsync, via DialogService.OpenAsync<DeactivateUserDialog>.
     The parent passes one parameter: UserDisplayName (string — the user's
     FullName). The dialog returns true if the user confirmed, false/null
     otherwise (matching the CancelConfirmationDialog.razor contract used by
     the sale-cancel flow).

   WHY A CUSTOM DIALOG (not JS confirm()):
     The previous implementation called window.confirm() with a long
     sentence, which gives a sparse native browser popup that:
       - Doesn't match the app's Radzen styling (looks foreign in both
         light/dark themes and breaks the visual flow on the admin pages).
       - Renders in the browser's locale (Chrome/Firefox show "This page
         says:" headers and OS-localized OK/Cancel buttons), not the
         app's en-US/fa-IR culture.
       - Has no friction against reflex confirmations — one click and
         the user is locked out.
     The user's spec asks for the same in-app popup pattern as the
     sale-cancel flow (CancelConfirmationDialog.razor), INCLUDING the
     3-second countdown on the confirm button. Rationale: even though
     deactivation is reversible (via Activate), it blocks the target
     user from logging in — which could be a mistake, and a 3-second
     pause is enough to catch a reflex click on the wrong row.

   LAYOUT (mirrors CancelConfirmationDialog.razor):
     - Warning heading (red, bold — "⚠️ Login will be blocked")
     - Body: three pieces — intro mentioning the user's name, an
       explanation that the user will be unable to log in (but their
       sales/history are preserved and the account can be reactivated),
       and a "please confirm" sentence.
     - Footer: "No, go back" (Secondary) + "Yes, I'm sure" (Danger).
       The Yes button is DISABLED for the first 3 seconds after the
       dialog opens, and shows the live countdown in its label.

   LIFECYCLE & ROBUSTNESS:
     - OnInitializedAsync starts a 3-iteration countdown loop with 1s
       sleep between iterations. Each iteration updates `countdown`
       and calls StateHasChanged so the button text re-renders.
     - A CancellationTokenSource is created up-front; if the user closes
       the dialog before the countdown finishes (e.g. clicks "No, go
       back" or hits the X), the Dispose() method cancels the CTS so
       the Task.Delay doesn't keep ticking in the background.
     - We catch BOTH OperationCanceledException (raised when the CTS is
       cancelled) AND ObjectDisposedException (raised if Dispose runs so
       fast that the next Task.Delay tries to access a disposed CTS).
     - The dialog is single-purpose — no Command dispatch here. The
       parent decides what to do based on the bool return value.

   LOCALIZATION:
     en-US + fa-IR resx files sit next to this razor file. Both
     render the countdown number via {0} so fa-IR shows Persian digits
     when the culture's digit substitution kicks in.
*@

@inject Radzen.DialogService DialogService
@inject IStringLocalizer<Components.Dialogs.DeactivateUserDialog.DeactivateUserDialog> Loc
@implements IDisposable

<div class="deactivate-user-dialog" style="padding: 1rem;">
    <RadzenStack Gap="1rem">
        <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.H6" Class="deactivate-user-dialog__warning">
            @Loc["WarningHeading"]
        </RadzenText>

        <RadzenText>
            @Loc["BodyIntro"]
            <strong>@UserDisplayName</strong>.
            @Loc["BodyBlock"]
        </RadzenText>

        <RadzenText>@Loc["BodyConfirm"]</RadzenText>

        <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" Gap="0.5rem" JustifyContent="JustifyContent.End">
            <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ButtonNo"]"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                          Click="() => DialogService.Close(false)" />
            <RadzenButton Text="@ButtonText"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Danger"
                          Disabled="@(countdown > 0)"
                          Click="() => DialogService.Close(true)" />
        </RadzenStack>
    </RadzenStack>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public string UserDisplayName { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    private int countdown = 3;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    // Button text shows the countdown while it's ticking, then becomes the
    // plain "Yes, I'm sure" once the button is enabled. The format string
    // is localized so fa-IR can render the digits in Persian if it wants to.
    private string ButtonText =>
        countdown > 0
            ? string.Format(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["ButtonYesCounting"], countdown)
            : Loc["ButtonYesReady"];

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        try
        {
            for (int i = 3; i > 0; i--)
            {
                countdown = i;
                await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
                // Capture the token locally so that even if Dispose races
                // ahead and nulls out _cts, we still have a valid reference
                // to pass to Task.Delay. (Dispose also calls Cancel first,
                // so a disposed-token race here surfaces as
                // OperationCanceledException, which we catch below.)
                var token = _cts.Token;
                await Task.Delay(1000, token);
            }
            countdown = 0;
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* user closed dialog before countdown finished */ }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* Dispose ran between iterations — same story */ }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        try
        {
            _cts?.Cancel();
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* already disposed — fine */ }
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}